      "id": "r2",
      "type": "stanza_note",
      "lines": [
        {
          "n": 1,
          "text": "optionally formed like that of mas. nouns; so-मूर्तये or र्तिने. See",
          "end": "space"
        },
        {
          "n": 2,
          "text": "Gr. § 69. Pan. VII. I. 74.",
          "end": "space"
        }
      ],
      "uncertain": false,
      "note": null
    },
    {
      "id": "r3",
      "type": "stanza_note",
      "lines": [
        {
          "n": 1,
          "text": "स्वानुभूति & —स्वानुभूति-self-perception, manifestation of Brahma",
          "end": "space"
        },
        {
          "n": 2,
          "text": "in contemplation. एक means ‘principal, chief’, rather than ‘sole’;",
          "end": "space"
        },
        {
          "n": 3,
          "text": "cf. the couplet. एकोल्पार्थे प्रधाने च &c. quoted in Gr. § 154; also",
          "end": "space"
        },
        {
          "n": 4,
          "text": "Amara ‘ एके मुख्यान्यकेवलाः । मान—मीयतेऽनेनेति मानम् from मा+ल्युट्",
          "end": "space"
        },
        {
          "n": 5,
          "text": "",
          "end": "space"
        },
        {
          "n": 6,
          "text": "( अन ), a measure; hence the means of arriving at correct",
          "end": "space"
        },
        {
          "n": 7,
          "text": "knowledge. In this sense the word प्रमाण is more common.",
          "end": "space"
        },
        {
          "n": 8,
          "text": "The prama'nas, according to the Vedantins, are प्रत्यक्षानुमानागमाः",
          "end": "space"
        },
        {
          "n": 9,
          "text": "प्रमाणानि to which the Naiyáyikas add उपमान. Brahma is specially",
          "end": "space"
        },
        {
          "n": 10,
          "text": "regarded as प्रत्यक्षप्रमाणगम्य ‘capable of being realised by percep-",
          "end": "hyphen"
        },
        {
          "n": 11,
          "text": "tion,’ although there are other means of knowing it such as",
          "end": "space"
        },
        {
          "n": 12,
          "text": "आगम &c. Another reading here is स्वानुभूत्येकसाराय which Mr.",
          "end": "space"
        },
        {
          "n": 13,
          "text": "Telang interpretes in two ways:-(1) to him who is the sole",
          "end": "space"
        },
        {
          "n": 14,
          "text": "essence of self-knowledge; or (2) to him whose sole (or principal)",
          "end": "space"
        },
        {
          "n": 15,
          "text": "essence is self-knowledge, preferring the former, as that, he",
          "end": "space"
        },
        {
          "n": 16,
          "text": "thinks, gives the अद्वैत view of the Veda'nta.",
          "end": "space"
        }
      ],
      "uncertain": false,
      "note": null
    },
    {
      "id": "r4",
      "type": "stanza_note",
      "lines": [
        {
          "n": 1,
          "text": "शान्ताय &c.—The dat. by नमःस्वस्तिस्वाहास्वधालंवषड्योगाच्च’ Pan.",
          "end": "space"
        },
        {
          "n": 2,
          "text": "II. 3. 16. शान्तं is one of the attributes of Brahma; cf. the S'ruti",
          "end": "space"
        },
        {
          "n": 3,
          "text": "निष्कलं निष्क्रियं शान्तं निरवद्यं निरञ्जनम् । ’ तेजसे–refers to Brahma and",
          "end": "space"
        },
        {
          "n": 4,
          "text": "not to the third element. Brahma is often called परं ज्योतिः in",
          "end": "space"
        },
        {
          "n": 5,
          "text": "the Upanishads. Comp. एष संप्रसादः.........परं ज्योतिरुपसम्पद्य &c.",
          "end": "space"
        },
        {
          "n": 6,
          "text": "Chhándogya; also the S'ruti तद्देवा ज्योतिषां ज्योतिः ( उपासते ).",
          "end": "space"
        },
        {
          "n": 7,
          "text": "For the idea comp. Panchadas'î, quoted in the com.",
          "end": "space"
        }
      ],
      "uncertain": false,
      "note": null
    },
    {
      "id": "r5",
      "type": "stanza_note",
      "lines": [
        {
          "n": 1,
          "text": "“For the idea may be compared Gregory Nyssen’s description",
          "end": "space"
        },
        {
          "n": 2,
          "text": "of the divine nature quoted by the late Mr. Mansel in his philo-",
          "end": "hyphen"
        },
        {
          "n": 3,
          "text": "sophy of the Conditioned ( p. 16 ):—It is neither in place nor",
          "end": "space"
        },
        {
          "n": 4,
          "text": "in time, but before these and above these in an unspeakable",
          "end": "space"
        },
        {
          "n": 5,
          "text": "manner, contemplated itself by itself through faith alone,",
          "end": "space"
        },
        {
          "n": 6,
          "text": "neither measured by ages, nor moving along with times.”",
          "end": "space"
        }
      ],
      "uncertain": false,
      "note": null
    },
    {
      "id": "r6",
      "type": "stanza_note",
      "lines": [
        {
          "n": 1,
          "text": "Sl. 2. The present S'loka is said by the commentators",
          "end": "space"
        },
        {
          "n": 2,
          "text": "( whose remarks are perhaps based on a tradition which is almost",
          "end": "space"
        },
        {
          "n": 3,
          "text": "universal) to refer to a family intrigue the incidental discovery",
          "end": "space"
        },
        {
          "n": 4,
          "text": "of which filled Bhartṛhari with disgust for worldly life. It is",
          "end": "space"
        }
      ],
      "uncertain": false,
      "note": null
    },
